\title{A Generalized Variance Formula for the Digits of $1/p$ in Base $b$}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-13}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The repeating expansion (repetend) of a unit fraction $1/p$ in an integer base $b$ is one of the oldest objects in elementary number theory, yet the statistics of its digits---their mean, and especially their variance---have classically been treated only for the extremal case of a \emph{full reptend}, where the base is a primitive root modulo $p$ and the repetend attains its maximal length $p-1$. We develop, from a single Euclidean division identity, an exact closed-form theory of the digit mean and variance valid for an \emph{arbitrary} repetend length $\ell$. Writing $S$ and $T$ for the sum and the sum of squares of the digits over one period, and $R,Q,C$ for three symmetric sums of the remainder orbit, we prove: $p\,S = (b-1)\,R$; $p^2\,T + 2b\,C = (b^2+1)\,Q$; and the master variance identity
\[
p^2\big(\ell\,T - S^2\big) = \ell\big((b^2+1)\,Q - 2b\,C\big) - (b-1)^2 R^2,
\]
which expresses the (scaled) variance $V = (\ell T - S^2)/\ell^2$ purely through the remainder-orbit sums. We further prove a Midy-type complementarity theorem for reflecting orbits, recover the classical mean value $\tfrac{b-1}{2}$ for full reptends, and disprove the naive conjecture that the digit mean is always $\tfrac{b-1}{2}$ (witness: the base-$2$ expansion of $1/7$). No primality hypothesis is needed; primality enters only when the orbit sums are interpreted as sums over the cyclic subgroup $\langle b\rangle \le (\Z/p\Z)^\times$, the gateway to a conjectural reformulation via generalized Bernoulli numbers of Dirichlet characters whose order divides $d = (p-1)/\ell$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Fix an integer base $b \ge 2$ and a modulus $p \ge 2$ with $\gcd(p,b) = 1$. Long division of $1$ by $p$ in base $b$ produces a purely periodic sequence of digits; the repeating block is the \emph{repetend}, and its length $\ell$ equals the multiplicative order $\ord_p(b)$ when $p$ is prime. The digits of such expansions have fascinated mathematicians since at least the eighteenth century: the cyclic-number property of $142857$, Midy's theorem that the two halves of a full-length decimal repetend sum to a string of nines, and the observation that the digits of a full-reptend prime average exactly $\tfrac{b-1}{2}$.

These classical facts are stated for \emph{full reptends}, where $b$ is a primitive root modulo the prime $p$ and $\ell = p-1$. When $\ord_p(b)$ is a proper divisor of $p-1$, the repetend is shorter and the clean statistical statements break down---the mean, in particular, need not be $\tfrac{b-1}{2}$.

We give an exact, elementary, fully general treatment. The mean and variance of the repetend digits, for \emph{any} period length $\ell$, are governed by three closed-form identities relating the digit sums $S,T$ to three symmetric sums $R,Q,C$ of the remainder orbit. The centerpiece is a single variance identity that eliminates the digits entirely. Every identity is derived from the Euclidean relation $b r_n = p d_n + r_{n+1}$ by summing and squaring over a period; no primality is used, so the identities hold verbatim for composite $p$ coprime to $b$. Section~\ref{sec:char} explains how, for prime $p$, the orbit sums become subgroup sums and connect to Dirichlet characters and generalized Bernoulli numbers.

\section{Setup and definitions}

\begin{definition}[Remainder orbit]
For integers $p,b$ define $r_n = \rem(p,b,n)$ by $r_0 = 1$ and $r_{n+1} = (b\cdot r_n)\bmod p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Digit sequence]
The $n$-th repetend digit is $d_n = \digit(p,b,n) = \left\lfloor b\,r_n / p\right\rfloor$.
\end{definition}

When $\gcd(p,b)=1$ the orbit $(r_n)$ is purely periodic, with minimal period $\ell = \ord_p(b)$ for prime $p$. A \emph{period} is any index $\ell$ with $r_\ell = r_0$; all sums below run over $k = 0,\dots,\ell-1$.

\begin{lemma}[Euclidean identity]\label{lem:euclid}
For all $n$, $\quad b\cdot r_n = p\cdot d_n + r_{n+1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
This is the defining relation of integer quotient and remainder: $d_n = \lfloor b r_n/p\rfloor$ and $r_{n+1} = (b r_n)\bmod p$, so $p\,d_n + r_{n+1} = p\lfloor b r_n/p\rfloor + (b r_n \bmod p) = b r_n$.
\end{proof}

Over one period of length $\ell$ define the \emph{digit sums} $S = \sum_k d_k$, $T = \sum_k d_k^2$, and the \emph{remainder-orbit sums}
\[
R = \sum_{k=0}^{\ell-1} r_k, \qquad Q = \sum_{k=0}^{\ell-1} r_k^2, \qquad C = \sum_{k=0}^{\ell-1} r_k\,r_{k+1}.
\]
The mean and variance of the digits are $\mu = S/\ell$ and $V = T/\ell - \mu^2 = (\ell T - S^2)/\ell^2$.

\begin{lemma}[Shift lemma]\label{lem:shift}
For any $f:\N\to\Z$ and any $n$,
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(k+1) = \Big(\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(k)\Big) + f(n) - f(0).
\]
In particular, over a period $r_\ell = r_0$ one has $\sum_{k=0}^{\ell-1} r_{k+1} = R$ and $\sum_{k=0}^{\ell-1} r_{k+1}^2 = Q$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The left side telescopes: $\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(f(k+1)-f(k)) = f(n)-f(0)$; add $\sum f(k)$. Applying it with $f=r$ (resp.\ $f=r^2$) and using $r_\ell = r_0$ cancels the boundary terms.
\end{proof}

\section{The digit-sum formula}

\begin{theorem}[Digit-sum identity]\label{thm:digitsum}
Over any period ($r_\ell = r_0$),
\[
b\cdot R = p\cdot S + R, \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad p\cdot S = (b-1)\cdot R.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Summing Lemma~\ref{lem:euclid} over $k=0,\dots,\ell-1$ gives $b\,R = p\,S + \sum_k r_{k+1}$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:shift} with $r_\ell = r_0$, $\sum_k r_{k+1} = R$. Hence $bR = pS + R$, and rearranging gives $pS = (b-1)R$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Digit mean]\label{cor:mean}
$\displaystyle \mu = \frac{S}{\ell} = \frac{(b-1)R}{p\,\ell}$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The digit sum-of-squares formula}

\begin{lemma}[Pointwise quadratic identity]\label{lem:sq}
For all $n$, $\quad p^2 d_n^2 + 2b\, r_n r_{n+1} + r_{n+1}^2 = b^2 r_n^2 + 2 r_{n+1}^2.$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Square Lemma~\ref{lem:euclid}: $b^2 r_n^2 = p^2 d_n^2 + 2 p d_n r_{n+1} + r_{n+1}^2$. Since $p d_n = b r_n - r_{n+1}$, we get $2 p d_n r_{n+1} = 2 b r_n r_{n+1} - 2 r_{n+1}^2$, hence $b^2 r_n^2 = p^2 d_n^2 + 2 b r_n r_{n+1} - r_{n+1}^2$. Rearranging yields the identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sum-of-squares identity]\label{thm:sumsq}
Over any period ($r_\ell = r_0$), $\quad p^2\cdot T + 2b\cdot C = (b^2+1)\cdot Q.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Sum Lemma~\ref{lem:sq} over the period: $p^2 T + 2b\,C + \sum_k r_{k+1}^2 = b^2 Q + 2\sum_k r_{k+1}^2$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:shift}, $\sum_k r_{k+1}^2 = Q$, so $p^2 T + 2b\,C = (b^2+1)Q$.
\end{proof}

\section{The generalized variance formula}

\begin{theorem}[Generalized variance identity]\label{thm:var}
For any period length $\ell$ ($r_\ell = r_0$),
\[
p^2\big(\ell\,T - S^2\big) = \ell\big((b^2+1)\,Q - 2b\,C\big) - (b-1)^2\,R^2,
\]
and consequently
\[
V = \frac{\ell T - S^2}{\ell^2} = \frac{\ell\big((b^2+1)Q - 2bC\big) - (b-1)^2 R^2}{p^2\,\ell^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sumsq}, $p^2 T = (b^2+1)Q - 2bC$, so $p^2\ell T = \ell((b^2+1)Q - 2bC)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:digitsum}, $pS = (b-1)R$, so $p^2 S^2 = (b-1)^2 R^2$. Subtracting gives the identity; dividing by $p^2\ell^2$ gives $V$.
\end{proof}

The right-hand side contains no digit quantities: the digit variance is a fixed polynomial in $R,Q,C$ and $p,b,\ell$, valid for every divisor $\ell$ of the period. This unifies the classically separate cases $\ell = p-1$ and $\ell = (p-1)/2$.

\begin{example}
For $1/7$ in base $10$: $\ell = 6$, $R = 21$, $Q = 91$, $C = 70$. The right side is $6(101\cdot 91 - 20\cdot 70) - 81\cdot 441 = 11025$, and the left side is $49(6\cdot 159 - 27^2) = 49\cdot 225 = 11025$. Thus $V = 225/36 = 25/4$.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[Full-reptend mean]\label{thm:fullmean}
Suppose $p \ne 0$ and $2R = p(p-1)$ (realized when $b$ is a primitive root modulo the prime $p$, so the remainders range over all of $\{1,\dots,p-1\}$). Then $2S = (b-1)(p-1)$, i.e.\ $\mu = \tfrac{b-1}{2}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:digitsum}, $pS = (b-1)R$, so $2pS = (b-1)\cdot 2R = (b-1)p(p-1)$. Cancelling $p\ne 0$ gives $2S = (b-1)(p-1)$; with $\ell = p-1$, $\mu = \tfrac{b-1}{2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Failure of the naive mean conjecture]\label{thm:notalways}
It is \emph{not} true that $2S = (b-1)\ell$ for every $p,b$ and period $\ell>0$. For $p=7$, $b=2$, $\ell=3$, the repetend of $1/7 = 0.\overline{001}$ has digits $0,0,1$, so $2S = 2 \ne 3 = (b-1)\ell$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The base-$2$ orbit of $1/7$ is $1,2,4,1$, so $\ell = 3$ is a period, with digits $d_0 = \lfloor 2/7\rfloor = 0$, $d_1 = \lfloor 4/7\rfloor = 0$, $d_2 = \lfloor 8/7\rfloor = 1$. Thus $S = 1$ and $2S = 2 \ne 3$.
\end{proof}

Here $\ord_7(2) = 3 < 6$, so $\{1,2,4\}$ is a proper subgroup of $(\Z/7\Z)^\times$ summing to $7$, not $21$; by Corollary~\ref{cor:mean} the mean is $\tfrac{1}{3} \ne \tfrac12$.

\section{Midy-type complementarity}

\begin{theorem}[Midy pairing]\label{thm:midy}
Let $p>0$. Suppose the orbit reflects with offset $m$: $r_{n+m} + r_n = p$ and $r_{n+m+1} + r_{n+1} = p$. Then $d_n + d_{n+m} + 1 = b$, i.e.\ $d_n + d_{n+m} = b-1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Add Lemma~\ref{lem:euclid} at $n$ and $n+m$: $b(r_n + r_{n+m}) = p(d_n + d_{n+m}) + (r_{n+1} + r_{n+m+1})$. The hypotheses give $b p = p(d_n + d_{n+m}) + p = p(d_n + d_{n+m}+1)$. Cancelling $p>0$ yields $b = d_n + d_{n+m} + 1$.
\end{proof}

For prime $p$ with even period $\ell$, taking $m = \ell/2$ (so $b^{\ell/2}\equiv -1$) gives the classical Midy theorem: opposite digits sum to $b-1$, e.g.\ $142+857 = 999$ in base ten.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Orbit generation.} Given $p,b$ with $\gcd(p,b)=1$: set $r\leftarrow 1$; repeatedly append $r$ and $\lfloor br/p\rfloor$, then set $r\leftarrow (br)\bmod p$, until $r=1$. The loop runs $\ell = \ord_p(b)$ times at $O(\log(bp))$ bit operations each, for a total of $O(\ell\log(bp))$.

\paragraph{Variance via orbit sums.} From the period, compute $R,Q,C,S,T$ in one pass, verify $pS=(b-1)R$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:digitsum}), $p^2T+2bC=(b^2+1)Q$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:sumsq}), and $p^2(\ell T - S^2) = \ell((b^2+1)Q - 2bC) - (b-1)^2R^2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:var}); return the exact rational $V = \big(\ell((b^2+1)Q-2bC) - (b-1)^2R^2\big)/(p^2\ell^2)$. All arithmetic is exact.

\section{Applications and discussion}

Theorem~\ref{thm:var} computes the variance of repetend digits from three integer orbit sums rather than the digits themselves---advantageous for long repetends. Corollary~\ref{cor:mean} and Theorem~\ref{thm:fullmean} show the mean equals $\tfrac{b-1}{2}$ exactly in the balanced-orbit case, so deviation of the mean detects that $b$ is not a primitive root modulo $p$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:notalways}). Theorem~\ref{thm:midy} isolates the exact reflection hypothesis behind the ``casting out nines'' folklore and shows it is base-independent. All identities of Sections~3--6 hold for any $p$ coprime to $b$; primality is invoked only to identify the orbit with a full subgroup and to guarantee reflection.

\section{Toward a character-theoretic form}\label{sec:char}

For prime $p$, the orbit is the cyclic subgroup $H = \langle b\rangle \le (\Z/p\Z)^\times$ of order $\ell$, and
\[
R = \sum_{h\in H} h, \qquad Q = \sum_{h\in H} h^2, \qquad C = \sum_{h\in H} h\cdot(bh\bmod p).
\]
With $d = (p-1)/\ell$, the subgroup indicator is $\ind_H(x) = \frac{\ell}{p-1}\sum_{\chi:\,\chi|_H=1}\chi(x)$, summed over the $d$ Dirichlet characters mod $p$ trivial on $H$; this expresses $R,Q,C$ through twisted power sums $\sum_x \chi(x)x^k$. For nonprincipal $\chi$, these equal $p\,B_{1,\chi}$ and (up to elementary corrections) $p^2 B_{2,\chi}$, the generalized Bernoulli numbers. Combining with the identities above would upgrade Theorem~\ref{thm:var} to a closed formula for the variance in terms of $B_{k,\chi}$ for $\chi$ of order dividing $d$, recovering the known $\ell = p-1$ and $\ell = (p-1)/2$ formulas. The only genuinely new analytic input is the base-dependent cross sum $C$.

\section{Future directions}

The present results establish the exact, character-free skeleton, valid for arbitrary $\ell$ and without primality. The remaining program is the analytic bridge of Section~\ref{sec:char}: (i) the subgroup orthogonality relation writing $\ind_H$ over the $d$ characters trivial on $H$; (ii) the identification of twisted power sums $\sum\chi(x)x^k$ with generalized Bernoulli numbers $B_{k,\chi}$; and (iii) the base-dependent cross sum $C$. Completing these would express the digit variance entirely through generalized Bernoulli numbers of characters of order dividing $d$, extending the classical formulas to every repetend length dividing $p-1$.

\section{Conclusion}

From the single relation $b r_n = p d_n + r_{n+1}$ we derived a complete exact theory of the mean and variance of the repetend digits of $1/p$ in base $b$: $pS = (b-1)R$, $p^2 T + 2bC = (b^2+1)Q$, and $p^2(\ell T - S^2) = \ell((b^2+1)Q - 2bC) - (b-1)^2 R^2$. These hold for arbitrary period length and without primality, subsume the classical full- and half-reptend results, formalize Midy-type complementarity, and disprove the naive universality of the mean $\tfrac{b-1}{2}$. The remainder-orbit sums $R,Q,C$ carry the digit statistics, and their subgroup structure marks where elementary arithmetic hands off to the analytic theory of Dirichlet characters.

